course: dictionary
lesson: 3361
title: Words that show how
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how something is done. Excitedly ends in -ly and comes from the word excited. It tells you how someone speaks or moves: with eager, happy energy.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «The two creatures excitedly explain the Lobster Quadrille, a seaside dance» gives you the seaside and the dance, but excitedly tells you the creatures are full of eager feeling about it.
explain: Writers often use excitedly to help you hear a voice or see a movement, such as «said excitedly» or «jumped up and down excitedly». It can also show nervous excitement in a frightening moment, when someone is thrilled and scared at once.
fact: Excitedly means in an excited way.
fact: Excitedly comes from the adjective excited, with -ly added.
fact: Excitedly means full of eager, happy energy or strong feeling.
fact: Writers use excitedly to show how characters speak or move, so readers can hear the voice.
fact: Excitedly can also show nervous excitement in a frightening moment.
exercise: «The two creatures excitedly explain the Lobster Quadrille, a seaside dance.» What does excitedly tell you about the creatures? | They are asleep and yawning | *They explain with eager, happy energy | They are drawing a map of the sea | They explain in a slow, bored way
exercise: Which word is the adverb made from the adjective excited? | excite | *excitedly | excitement | exciting
exercise: A child «jumped up and down excitedly» on the bed. What is the child feeling? | *Thrilled and full of energy | Angry with the bed | Bored and very tired | Hungry for lunch
test: «They chattered excitedly about the seaside trip.» What does chattered excitedly show? | They were silent and still | *They talked fast with happy, eager feeling | They were cross and shouted at each other | They were falling asleep
test: «She crept excitedly towards the door, her heart pounding.» What feeling does excitedly show here? | *Eager feeling mixed with nerves in a scary moment | Sleepy and slow | Happy and safe at home | Calm and bored
exam: Why might a writer use excitedly instead of just saying the character was happy? | To hide the feeling from the reader | Because excitedly means sleepy | To make the sentence longer for no reason | *To show how the character speaks or moves, so readers can hear the voice
